u have  issues with you know sourcing some  materials  um have a plan of what you're going to  do to respond to the agency if they ask  you about that  um a couple of other things they were  asking for were cfas for all raw  materials  um this wasn't so surprising to me  because if if you dealt with the EMA EMA  ask for a lot of CMA so I was used to  this but I do think that this is  possibly in response to the supply  issues they they want to know where your  where you're sourcing your raw materials  from and who your vendors are so they  wanted to see everything and again when  they were talking about raw materials  they were talking about everything they  were talking about you know bags and um  you know bags and bottles and tips and  um and even you know as well as reagents  and as well as you know critical raw  materials so they were talking about  everything  um the other thing that they asked for  which was again surprising was uh  commercial shipping I said sop  um the commercial shipping SLP  for us doesn't it it's not necessarily  surprising because we're shipping  patient cells from one place to the next  and they want to make sure that you know  from a safety perspective how are they  how are we ensuring that the you know  the material is safe Etc so that makes  sense  um but it just reminds me  um and we'll get into a little bit I'll  touch on a little bit later but just  reminds me when they start asking for  Sops it's like  um when I first started in regulatory  um the agency when they when you would  do a submission they would ask for all  your Sops they would ask for everything  and so this reminded me of this where  they're sort of going backwards and  they're saying Hey give us give us all  your Sops give us everything so it's  just a call out just interesting  um okay  oh additionally  um they asked for  um again this is the facility  information additionally they ask for a  lot of facility information so they  asked about raw material qualification  program they asked about the aseptic uh  process simulation data and results  equipment and cleaning validations  environmental monitoring environmental  monitoring was big they asked they asked  for to know what the excursions were  during your APS runs or during your PPQ  runs  and so anyway so they asked for a lot of  information from the facility and again  this was  all prior to setting foot on the  facility so this was a little bit  unusual and again it may be a situation  where the agency was trying to  sort of do a virtual visit and handle it  that way as opposed to doing  um you know getting this information on  site at the time when they did that  inspection  so the inspection so um it wasn't the  day of there was this each of these  inspections was basically a five-day  inspection at each facility  um both sites were inspected in 2021  there was a small on-site presence from  from us because we you know because of  coveted restrictions at the facility we  just couldn't have a large uh  contingency of people there  um so we did decide decide to create  essentially a war room  um at a facility that was or at a hotel  that was very close to the manufacturing  facility so we were very close in  proximity and time zone to everything  that was taking place at the facility  um so we did that  um  as I said oh the inter this is another  interesting thing  um they the agency agreed that we had  two products being manufactured at a  facility and generally they would come  in and inspect each product separately  but they agreed to uh  they agreed to inspect  each facility one time for both products  so they just wanted to see one of our  products being manufactured and they  figured the other product was  manufactured in a similar manner so that  it would be representative of it  um and then the other thing that they  did so anyway so they combined the  inspections so rather than having  possibly four inspections two  inspections at each site we only had two  inspections and then the other thing  they did at the drug substance drug  product manufacturer is they combined a  GMP inspection and a pli inspection into  one and so I found this interesting  because  it's been my experience in the past that  the agency has taken as many  opportunities as they can to come in and  inspect a facility and really get get in  there see what's going on and make sure  that they're you know complying with the  CFR  um and here we have a situation where  they uh easily agreed to combining  inspections on several occasions and  that may be a reflection of  of the pandemic and they didn't want to  spend too much time at the facility and  they knew it was challenging or it may  be uh it may have just been coincidence  I don't know which but um but it could  be a combination of the fact that or it  could be a result of the of the pandemic  itself  um and then lastly the CMO led the you  know this the inspection the inspection  lasted about five days there were no  major observations at either facility  um  and uh so that was good  um an overview of the inspection itself  um it was uh the inspection itself was  Heavy GMP Focus  um very light product questions so we  got maybe like 10 of the questions we  had during the inspection were product  related and those were focused around  product release but the majority of  everything else was was GMP focused  questions so  um focusing on the  sorry apologies um Quality Management  Systems lens Inventory management  traceability Inventory management again  I felt that they were really harping on  the inventory management system  particularly because they're worried  about the supply chain issues  um and and they really wanted to know  how the facilities are making sure that  they have product you know basically  have they have all the raw materials and  everything they need to manufacture  um the other things that they focused on  for us were traceability chain of  identity in particular and that makes  sense again because we're we're using  human cells we're giving them back to  the patients so they want to make sure  they want to know what the traceability  is uh the chain of identity and make  sure that everything is being done  correctly the other questions they had  were about capacity planning they wanted  to know at the facility itself you know  how are you going to handle uh  commercial manufacturing do you have the  person you know capacity for personnel  do you have the capacity do you need  another site you can open up another you  know room to manufacturer Etc so they  ask questions about that  and then another interesting one which  was kind of funny they asked a lot of  questions about container closure  Integrity testing and this one caught us  off a surprise we're like why are they  asking these questions  um and it turned out that um one of our  bag manufacturers who had a dnf with the  FDA had pulled their DMF so the FDA  couldn't get information that they were  looking for so they came to us for the  information and we learned we said oh  the dmf's been pulled and so we actually  had to go to the manufacturer get all  the information from them and then give  it to the FTA so it all worked out but  it was at first it was a little strange  like why are they asking all these  questions but  um well we didn't know that our bag  manufacturer had retracted its DMF  so  all right so  takeaways or Lessons Learned so  getting to the end here  um  lessons learned from the from the from  the whole uh endeavor  um the FDA is looking for a very  granular Blas  um they they want a lot of information  in the Blas and if they're not getting  in the Blas they're actually using the  IRS to to  for even additional information  um and this is okay but  um and it's just fine I mean the FDA  obviously can ask whatever it wants and  we need to provide it  um but you know going back to my earlier  statement I like to be prepared so I  like to know you know know what the FDA  is going to be asking and what they want  from us and uh and I've always aired on  the side of giving the FDA just enough  so that they can you know they can ask  questions but you know not so much that  you're you're burdened with information  requests and uh so my you know one of my  fears is if you give FDA everything  they're going to ask all these questions  if you give them too much they're going  to just keep on asking these questions  and it's like If You Give a Mouse a  Cookie You Give a Mouse a Cookie the  mouse is going to say well how about  some milk and then you give the mouse  some milk and the milk and the mouse is  going to say well I need to I need a nap  kit now to wipe the milk off my chin and  they just keep asking these questions so  you don't want to give them too much you  want to have a balance  um but it's challenging if you don't  know where the bar is you don't know  where what the FDA is  um is asking so  um  need to learn in the future from the  agency is where their bar is you know  what what what are they what do they  really want  um rather than continuing to answer all  these IRS  um but so the agency has provided some  new guidance  um and and they I think the last one  that came out was March for Gene  therapies but um but we need to we need  some more we need we need a little bit  more granular detail about what they're  looking for so the industry should  continue to  um ask the agency to continue to update  its uh guidances so that we can  understand so we can prepare and prepare  understand what they're what they're  asking for  so  um all right  and then um and then takeaways takeaways  from from this experience uh plis  continue to focus you know heavily on  quality and and GMP  um not  the plis don't Focus as much on the  product related questions  um the product related questions are  definitely coming through the majority  of the IRS so they're using IRS to get a  lot of the product related information  and it a